A beloved Southern autumn tradition featured by Southern Living. Rather than relying on applesauce or purée, this vintage sheet cake is packed with raw, thinly sliced Granny Smith apple wedges that soften into tender pockets of fruit inside a spiced cinnamon crumb. Topped with a luscious layer of tangy cream cheese frosting and toasted Southern pecans.

*Adapted from [Southern Living](https://www.southernliving.com/recipes/fresh-apple-cake).*

## Ingredients

### Fresh Apple Cake Batter
- 1 ½ cups chopped pecans, divided (1 cup for batter, ½ cup for garnish)
- ½ cup (1 stick) unsalted butter, melted and cooled slightly
- 2 cups granulated sugar
- 2 large eggs, room temperature
- 1 teaspoon pure vanilla extract
- 2 cups all-purpose flour
- 2 teaspoons ground cinnamon
- 1 teaspoon baking soda
- 1 teaspoon kosher salt
- 4 large Granny Smith apples, peeled, cored, and cut into thin bite-sized wedges (about 4 cups)
- Nonstick baking spray with flour

### Cream Cheese Frosting
- 8 oz cream cheese, softened to room temperature
- ½ cup (1 stick) unsalted butter, softened to room temperature
- 3 cups confectioners' sugar (powdered sugar), sifted
- 1 teaspoon pure vanilla extract

## Instructions

1. **Toast the Pecans and Prep Pan:** Preheat your oven to 350°F (175°C). Spread the chopped pecans across a rimmed baking sheet in an even layer. Bake for 5 to 7 minutes, stirring once halfway through, until fragrant and lightly toasted. Remove and let cool. Lightly coat a 9x13-inch metal or glass baking pan with baking spray.
2. **Whisk the Wet Base:** In a large mixing bowl, combine the melted butter, granulated sugar, eggs, and pure vanilla extract. Whisk vigorously for 1 to 2 minutes until glossy and well combined.
3. **Incorporate Dry Ingredients:** Add the all-purpose flour, ground cinnamon, baking soda, and kosher salt directly into the bowl. Switch to a sturdy wooden spoon or rubber spatula and stir until the flour is completely incorporated. The batter will be noticeably dense and heavy.
4. **Fold in Fresh Apples and Nuts:** Dump the peeled and thinly sliced Granny Smith apples and 1 cup of the toasted pecans into the bowl (save the remaining ½ cup of pecans for the frosting garnish). Fold patiently until every apple slice is coated in batter. As the fruit sits, the sugar will begin drawing out natural apple juices, slightly loosening the batter.
5. **Bake to Golden:** Scrape the batter into the prepared 9x13-inch pan, smoothing it out into the edges and corners with your spatula. Bake at 350°F for 45 to 50 minutes. The cake is done when the top is springy to the touch and a wooden toothpick inserted into the center comes out clean of batter (it may hit a soft apple, which is fine).
6. **Cool Completely:** Set the cake pan on a wire cooling rack and allow it to cool down completely to room temperature before frosting (about 1.5 to 2 hours), otherwise the cream cheese frosting will melt.
7. **Whip the Frosting:** In a medium bowl using an electric hand mixer (or stand mixer fitted with the paddle), beat the room-temperature cream cheese and softened butter on medium speed for 2 to 3 minutes until light, creamy, and lump-free. Gradually add the powdered sugar in portions, beating on low speed until incorporated, then increase speed to medium-high. Add the vanilla extract and beat for another 60 seconds until satiny and spreadable.
8. **Frost and Garnish:** Dollop the frosting over the cooled cake and spread evenly with an offset spatula. Scatter the reserved ½ cup toasted chopped pecans over the top. Slice into 15 generous squares and serve.

## Notes

- **Best Apples for Baking:** Tart Granny Smith apples hold their structure beautifully without turning to mush, offering a sharp contrast to the sweet cinnamon crumb and cream cheese frosting. Honeycrisp or Braeburn also work wonderfully.
- **Storage:** Because of the cream cheese frosting, store any leftover cake covered in the refrigerator for up to 5 days. Let chilled slices sit on the counter for 15 minutes before serving so the cake crumb relaxes.
